# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Paragrafo di atmosfera aggiunto a ogni storia (porta il totale a ~115-120 parole con gli indizi)."""
EXTRAS = {
1: "The vicar twists his hands. 'Ambrose always arrived early and always complained about the draught.' Outside the window, rain taps the glass, and a lone magpie watches the garden. 'Please look carefully,' he adds. 'Small things matter in Marrowby, and this was a very dear one.'",
2: "The forge is hot and smells of smoke. A young apprentice hovers by the door, pretending not to listen. 'Ask him about the Tuesday order,' the smith mutters. You make a note, and tuck a sooty thumbprint into your pocketbook for later.",
3: "Behind the counter, a wall of little compartments is stuffed with letters. Mabel taps one with a fingernail. 'He asked to borrow my pen, and then he asked me the time. Twice. Ambrose, of all people, asking the time!' She shakes her head sadly.",
4: "A draught makes the shadows move. Somewhere above, the organ gives a sigh. The sexton lowers his voice. 'Folk say these stairs are haunted, but ghosts do not drop things. Somebody real was here, and somebody real got nervous.' You step carefully and start to count the steps.",
5: "The doctor has a habit of noticing hands, shoes and hems. 'Whoever it was wore good leather shoes,' she says, 'and kept turning to look at the church clock. I assumed they were late for the last bus. Now I am not so sure.'",
6: "The river slides past, black and silent. The ferryman rubs his whiskers. 'Heard a splash, too, though I can't say it was anything but a duck. Ducks don't whistle, mind.' You note the time, and the odd smell of paraffin on the planks.",
7: "Wilf Ottley folds his arms and glares at the gate as if it had insulted him. 'Thirty years I have grown beans here, and never needed a bolt.' A robin hops along the fence. 'Somebody wants my shed shut, and I mean to find out why.'",
8: "Bunting flaps overhead and the smell of lemon icing hangs in the air. Nell Crumpet, red in the face, wrings her hands. 'Five years I have won, and not once have I cheated! Look at the table. Somebody moved the cards, the cake knives, and the prize.'",
9: "The reading room is silent apart from the soft ticking of a wall clock, one of Ambrose's own. Ines straightens a pile of already straight books. 'He asked to see an old document,' she says, 'and he asked me to lock the door. I thought he was joking.'",
10: "Hattie hands you a mug of tea and a pair of muddy gloves. 'The prints go right to the door and back,' she says, 'and one is much deeper than the other, as if the digger carried something heavy. A rosebud was crushed under the gate. I do not forgive that.'",
11: "Dust floats in the sunbeams across the stage. The tuner polishes his glasses. 'In thirty years I have never found anything under a piano but pencils and pennies,' he says. 'Yet this note was written in Ambrose Wickes's own hand, and signed with a single letter.'",
12: "Lady Cordelia stands at the stable gate with her arms folded. 'The horses were calm,' she says, 'which they never are when strangers visit. So the rider was someone they knew.' The groom coughs politely and looks at his boots. 'Well, madam, that is rather the problem.'",
13: "A single lamp glows on the platform and a cat naps on the bench. The stationmaster adjusts his cap. 'The man looked right, then left, then left again. He stared at the church clock the whole time, as if he were counting down to something.'",
14: "Music from the dance floor floats down the corridor. The attendant, a nervous girl with a pencil behind her ear, twists a handkerchief. 'I only looked away once,' she says, 'when somebody knocked over a tray. And I did not see who it was, honestly.'",
15: "The ladder creaks and the stone walls breathe cold. Dust lies thick on every step but a few, and these have been scuffed by recent feet. The vicar mops his forehead. 'I fear the tower has told us a secret,' he sighs, 'if only we can read it.'",
16: "Chandeliers tinkle overhead as Tobias glides between the shelves, straightening a vase here, a frame there. 'I am an open book,' he says warmly. You notice he has not once looked at the shop's tall clock, and that it is the only one with the wrong time.",
17: "The ovens are still warm and the shop smells of cinnamon. Nell shows you a rolling pin lying on the floor, and a sack of flour spilled across the tiles. 'Not a mouse,' she says darkly. 'Mice do not wear boots.' You crouch to study the print.",
18: "A cold wind combs the barley and sets the field whispering. The farmer leans on his stick. 'In forty years, nothing like this has happened. Not a fox, not a poacher, and certainly not a ghost.' He squints at the horizon. 'Someone knows my field better than I do.'",
19: "The loft smells of grain and warm dust, and a dozen grey heads turn to look at you. The Major clasps his hands behind his back like a general. 'The scroll bore only five words,' he says, 'and I memorized none of them. I shall never forgive myself.'",
20: "Sunlight pours through tall windows over jars of brushes and rags. The painter wipes his hands on a rag. 'Lady Cordelia hated the portrait, you know. Said I made her look worried. I told her I painted what I saw.' He gives a thin smile, and glances at the door.",
21: "A clock ticks softly on the mantel, one of Ambrose's finest. Lady Cordelia stands with her back to you, looking into the empty fireplace. 'I have told the constable all I know,' she says. 'Which is nothing, naturally.' But her hand is trembling, ever so slightly.",
22: "Stalls spill onto the cobbles, heaped with apples and ribbons. A boy sells flowers by the fountain and a goat eats somebody's hat. The spice seller wipes her hands. 'He was a tall man with a polite voice and ink on his fingers,' she says. 'Smelled of beeswax.'",
23: "A bell tinkles as you step in, and jars glow on every shelf like jewels. Mr Pym polishes his half-moon spectacles. 'I counted the jars at closing,' he says, 'and I counted them again at opening. One had been refilled with pebbles. Who plays such a trick?'",
24: "A heron lifts from the reeds, offended. The curate's wife dabs her forehead. 'We left for only ten minutes,' she says, 'to watch the cricket. When we came back, the sandwiches had vanished and a note lay on the grass. It said only: Ask about the will.'",
25: "A rusty bell hangs by the door and the brass pole gleams. Captain Moss tests the siren with a long, mournful note. 'Everything here is checked every morning,' he says, 'so someone had a key, and plenty of nerve. Nobody just wanders into a fire station.'",
26: "Gulls wheel overhead though the sea is miles away, and nobody can say why. The sailor chuckles. 'I know a good boat from a bad one, and that one left in too much of a hurry,' he says. 'Seen it, I did. A hooded figure at the oars, and no lamp lit.'",
27: "Bolts of cloth lean against the wall in every shade of grey. Mr Dunnock measures the air with his hands, a lifelong habit. 'Ambrose was a stubborn customer,' he says. 'He wanted pockets inside the lining, deep ones. Said they were for his papers. I thought no more of it.'",
28: "The dome creaks open onto a sky full of stars. The old schoolmaster hands you a cold flask of cocoa. 'The shop lamp flickered three times,' he says, 'like a signal. And a few minutes later, I saw a figure carrying a long box towards the churchyard.'",
29: "The kettle whistles on the stove and a dog snores under the desk. Constable Harcourt flips through his notebook. 'This is the third visitor since Monday who forgot to sign,' he says. 'Everyone in Marrowby swears they have nothing to hide. That is the suspicious part.'",
30: "Six chairs form a perfect circle around the empty table. Mabel pours tea into pretty cups and sighs. 'Every square tells a story, you know. Ambrose's waistcoat scrap was the final one, and we were just about to sew the last border. It is all so cruel.'",
31: "A cheerful tune lingers in the air, and a plate of biscuits sits untouched. The leader, Mr Hobday, taps his baton against a music stand. 'It sat on the shelf by the window,' he says. 'Ambrose said it made him feel eighty years younger. Imagine stealing that.'",
32: "The smell of fried bacon drifts from the kitchen and a grandfather clock chimes nine. Mrs Plover sets her lips in a thin line. 'Nobody leaves my table half-fed,' she says. 'And nobody walks off with my crockery. He was in a rush, he was, and he took the back stairs.'",
33: "Rows of rosettes flutter on the judges' table and the air smells of grass and honey. Hattie fusses over a vase of lilies. 'The swap happened between dusk and dawn,' she says. 'Whoever did it knew precisely which one was the champion. That is no stranger.'",
34: "Glass cabinets glitter in the half-light. Row upon row of buttons, badges and brass catch the sun. The Major clears his throat. 'I put every item here with my own two hands,' he says. 'I can tell you which one is wrong from across the room.'",
35: "The woods hum with insects and the smell of damp leaves. A path winds between ancient trunks, scattered with twigs and moss. The constable crouches, brushing leaves from a footprint. 'A man's boot,' he says. 'Size nine. Too neat for a poacher, too heavy for a vicar.'",
36: "Tins of biscuits and sacks of flour line the shelves, and the smell of tea and soap fills the air. Mr Pickering turns the pages with a sigh. 'Eleven years of honest sums,' he says. 'And then this. Somebody wanted Ambrose's name to say something it never said.'",
37: "Rows of glass jars gleam on the trestles, each with a neat handwritten label. The room is warm and sweet and full of cross voices. 'We have eleven judges,' Nell sighs, 'and ten of them have just walked out. Whoever did this wanted the whole day spoiled.'",
38: "Pots bubble and the kitchen is full of steam and chatter. The teacher, a stout woman in a spotted apron, taps her wooden spoon. 'The page has gone, but the grease stains remain on the folder,' she says. 'The thief had buttery fingers, and a very good appetite.'",
39: "The green is empty now, apart from the sheep. Long shadows creep across the pitch. The umpire pats his pockets again and again, as if the missing item might leap out. 'Without it, nobody could stop the match,' he says, 'and that match was important, I can tell you.'",
40: "Every wall is covered in clocks, all silent, all stopped at seven minutes past twelve. Cogs and springs lie across the bench in neat rows. The constable lowers his voice. 'I wound them all again myself, but the time came back the same. Strange, is it not?'",
41: "The great sails turn slowly in the breeze and the old beams groan like a ship. The miller brushes flour from his moustache. 'That gear was greased on the night of the murder,' he says. 'I know it was. And I did not do it. Somebody used my mill for secret work.'",
42: "Mist curls around the yew trees and the church clock strikes the hour with a cough. The sexton leans on his spade. 'Nobody has cleaned that grave in a year,' he says, 'yet the flowers on it are fresh each morning. I have never seen who brings them.'",
43: "A heavy door groans open and cold air rushes out. Inside, the brick walls glisten and every sound echoes. The gardener lights a stub of wick with a trembling hand. 'My grandfather used to say this place kept secrets,' he whispers. 'I never believed him until today.'",
44: "The band strikes up a waltz and couples spin across the polished boards. Lady Cordelia dabs her cheeks with a lace handkerchief. 'I felt it go,' she says, 'a tiny tug, and then nothing at all. Whoever found it did not hand it in, and that tells you something.'",
45: "The fire crackles in the hearth and the beams are black with age. Fergus rubs a glass until it squeaks. 'Ambrose never lost at this game,' he says. 'He lost that night, on purpose, and he looked terribly pleased about it. I have never understood why.'",
46: "The curtain was red velvet and the lights were dim. Children giggled at the front, and the Great Alfredo bowed low. Now he mops his brow backstage. 'A good magician never loses a prop,' he says gravely. 'A good magician is robbed only by a better one.'",
47: "Dust sheets cover the furniture like sleeping ghosts. The air smells of mothballs and stage paint, and a mannequin in a crown leers from the corner. The director snaps open a fan. 'The cloak was moved,' she says, 'and that, my dear, is the sign of a thorough searcher.'",
48: "Paper lanterns glow along the terrace and the lawn is still littered with petals. Lady Cordelia keeps a long list of guests, and a longer one of those she did not ask. Hattie lowers her voice. 'There was one name,' she says, 'I never expected to see.'",
49: "The steps are worn and slippery, and the air smells of oak and old grapes. Fergus lifts a lamp, and rows of dusty bottles wink back. 'Every bottle has its place,' he says, 'and every place has its bottle. One was moved from the second shelf, and that is the one I worry about.'",
50: "The museum is quiet as a church. Glass cases shine and a stuffed owl glares from a beam. The curator straightens a label that was already straight. 'Everyone in Marrowby has been in here this month,' she says, 'and everyone has been to see the clockmaker's display. Funny, that.'",
}
